From 94a34ba0a672dedf7b09f1c0571350a722e59273 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: wray-lee <wray-lee@outlook.com>
Date: Thu, 15 Oct 2026 05:44:56 +0900
Subject: [PATCH 1/2] feat(slack): stream outbound replies while they are
 generated

Slack was the only streaming-capable channel without an outbound stream
sink, so replies only appeared once the model had finished. Add
`channels.slack.accounts.<id>.streaming` (default false) and wire the
channel into the existing `.chunk`/`.final` outbound staging:

- In a thread, use Slack's native AI streaming methods:
  chat.startStream, throttled chat.appendStream deltas, then
  chat.stopStream.
- Elsewhere, and whenever Slack rejects the native methods, post one
  message with chat.postMessage and edit it with throttled chat.update.

Deltas are coalesced to at most one API call per second per turn so each
method stays inside its rate-limit tier, and tool-call markup is stripped
before anything is sent. A permanent error (missing_scope,
channel_type_not_supported, invalid_thread_ts, ...) disables the native
path for the account so later turns go straight to edited messages.

`/reasoning stream` now also shows the thinking live in the draft on
Slack and Telegram: the daemon forwards `<think>` blocks (TagFilter
passthrough mode) only for sessions that stream reasoning and only to
channels that render them, and the draft presenters render them as `>`
quotes. The delivered reply is unchanged - the provider still strips
think blocks from the final content, and `.off`/`.on` previews stay
stripped as before. A Slack turn that streamed reasoning rewrites the
stopped message in place instead of posting the reply twice.

Validation: `zig build test --summary all` (7555 passed, 0 leaked) and
`zig fmt --check` on the changed files, both in the Zig 0.16.0 container.
